  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows one more potent MCV ejecting out of \nIowa early this morning. Regional radar mosaic currently shows a \nbowing segment but mesoanalysis indicates very limited shear and \ntherefore unlikely to pose a severe risk. Once again our current \nenvironment is very stable but another round of strengthening \nnocturnal LLJ will advect higher theta-e air back into the area early \nthis morning. This should support at least some elevated convection \nroughly 08-15Z this morning. Convection will likely tend to outpace \nbetter surface-based instability but there's at least some low \nchances that a slightly later passage could allow some diurnal \ncontribution in the 14-17Z timeframe that would yield a gusty wind \nthreat for our far E/NE counties. This is suggested by some runs of \nthe HRRR but still think it will be difficult to get sufficient \ninstability that far NE that early to support severe storms. As for \nthe hydro threat...best overall rain chances look to be in our \nnorthern CWA and storms will likely be much more progressive and \nscattered than in recent days due to the current synoptic setup. The \nthreat of true \"heavy\" rain is very low but susceptibility to even \nmoderate rain in our southern CWA is still high. Given this and some \nlingering uncertainty on where exactly storms track later this \nmorning plan to just let the existing flood watch ride for now.\n\nAttention this afternoon and evening then turns to very broad/ \ndiffuse cold frontal passage. Ample low level moisture and some \nsteepening midlevel lapse rates generate over 2000 j/kg of SBCAPE in \nour southern zones this afternoon and 25-30kts of deep-layer shear \nwould suggest a threat for organized convection. The problem is the \nforcing is just very weak. We actually have some shortwave ridging \nin the wake of this morning MCV that persists well into the evening \nand low level convergence is virtually nil. Hi-res CAM's \nsubsequently show very isolated convection this afternoon and \nevening. Can't entirely rule out a stray strong storm in our \nsouth given the instability and shear parameters but risk \nappears very low at this point.\n\nFinally we return to quiet weather for Monday and Tuesday. The next \nchance of storms arrives Tue night. A deep upper low is forecast to \ncross northern Ontario with modest height falls extending into the \nGreat Lakes region. Associated fast midlevel flow yields some \nimpressive shear but instability will be in doubt for our area. \nGeneral WNW flow ahead of the front limits theta-e recovery and \nnighttime arrival limits the diurnal contribution. An earlier \narrival could yield a threat for stronger storms in our far NW Tue \nevening but chances for that currently appear low. This front will \nbe slow moving and rain chances extend through the day Wed but \nlackluster midlevel lapse rates and low level moisture return \nsuggest a low risk for both severe weather and heavy rain given the \ncurrent timing.\n\nForecast confidence isn't high for the end of the week given some \nfairly large model differences but there is a general lean toward \ncooler, drier, and more tranquil weather.",
